// 12 ── PRODUCT COMPARISON
export const COMPARE: Art = { name: '12 product comparison', site: 'https://www.oakandloomflooring.example',
  brief: { targetQuery: 'laminate vs vinyl flooring', searchIntent: 'compare', relatedQuestions: ['Which is better, laminate or vinyl flooring?', 'Is vinyl flooring more waterproof than laminate?', 'Which is cheaper, laminate or vinyl?', 'Which lasts longer, laminate or vinyl flooring?'] },
  body: { title: 'Laminate vs vinyl flooring: which should you choose?', seoTitle: 'Laminate vs vinyl flooring: cost, water and wear compared',
    metaDescription: 'Laminate vs vinyl flooring: we compare price, water resistance, comfort and lifespan room by room, so you can choose the right floor first time.',
    excerpt: 'Two floors that look alike and behave very differently.', slug: 'laminate-vs-vinyl-flooring',
    intro: `From across the showroom they are hard to tell apart. Both come as click-together planks, both copy oak or stone convincingly, and both cost a fraction of the real thing. Yet the laminate vs vinyl flooring question is the one we are asked more than any other, because under the surface they are completely different products.\n\nThis guide compares them on the things that matter once the floor is down: water, wear, comfort and cost.`,
    sections: sec([
      ['What each one is made of', `Laminate is a wood product. Its core is high-density fibreboard, with a photograph of timber on top and a hard, clear wear layer over that. It feels solid underfoot and the best boards have a texture that follows the grain in the picture.\n\nLuxury vinyl tile, usually shortened to LVT, is plastic all the way through. A printed layer sits on a flexible or rigid vinyl core, again under a clear wear layer. Because there is no wood in it, there is nothing to swell.\n\nThat single difference explains almost everything that follows.`],
      ['Is vinyl flooring more waterproof than laminate?', `Yes, and by a long way. Vinyl is waterproof. You can mop it, spill on it and fit it in a bathroom without worry, provided the subfloor is sound.\n\nStandard laminate is not. Water that gets into the joints soaks into the fibreboard, and the edges of the boards lift and stay lifted. Modern "water-resistant" laminate has sealed edges and will shrug off a spill for a day or so, which is fine for a kitchen. However, we would still not put it in a bathroom or a utility room, and most makers will not guarantee it there.`],
      ['Which is cheaper, laminate or vinyl?', `Laminate, as a rule. Decent boards start at about £15 a square metre and very good ones cost £25 to £35. Click vinyl starts nearer £25 and good rigid-core ranges run from £35 to £50. Glue-down LVT can cost less for the tiles themselves, but it needs a perfectly smooth subfloor, so the preparation often costs more than the floor.\n\nFitting costs are similar for the click versions of both, at around £12 to £18 a square metre. For a typical living room of 20 square metres, expect roughly £700 to £900 fitted in laminate and £900 to £1,300 in vinyl.`],
      ['Comfort, warmth and noise', `Vinyl is the quieter and warmer of the two. It has a little give, so it is kind to knees and to dropped mugs, and it does not have the hollow tap that cheap laminate makes under hard shoes. A good underlay improves laminate a great deal, so do not economise on it.\n\nBoth work over underfloor heating. Check the maximum surface temperature in the maker's instructions, which is normally 27 degrees.`],
      ['Which lasts longer, laminate or vinyl flooring?', `In a dry room, good laminate. Its wear layer is extremely hard, so it resists scratches from dog claws and dining chairs better than most vinyl. Look for a rating of AC4 or above for a busy household. Fifteen to twenty-five years is realistic.\n\nVinyl is softer, so it dents under heavy furniture and can be scratched by grit. On the other hand, it will not be ruined by a leaking dishwasher. A wear layer of 0.5mm will last twenty years in a home.\n\nNeither can be sanded and refinished. When the surface has gone, the floor is replaced.`],
      ['Which is better, laminate or vinyl flooring?', `It depends on the room.\n\n- Bathrooms, utility rooms and wet rooms: vinyl, every time.\n- Kitchens: vinyl, or a water-resistant laminate if you mop up promptly.\n- Living rooms, bedrooms and halls: laminate gives you more for your money.\n- Conservatories: check the specification, because some vinyl moves in strong sun.\n- Rented property: vinyl is more forgiving of tenants.\n\nPlenty of our customers use both: laminate through the living areas and a matching vinyl in the kitchen and bathroom. You can see how the two sit together in our [room gallery](https://www.oakandloomflooring.example/gallery).`],
    ]),
    conclusion: `Choose vinyl where there is water and laminate where there is not, and buy the best wear layer you can afford in either. If you are still undecided, take a sample of each home, leave them on the floor for a week and spill a cup of tea on both.`,
    cta: { label: 'Order free samples', url: 'https://www.oakandloomflooring.example/samples' },
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Can you put laminate in a kitchen?', a: 'Yes, if you choose a water-resistant range and wipe up spills the same day. Standing water from a leak will still damage it.' },
      { q: 'Does vinyl flooring look cheap?', a: 'Not any more. Good LVT has a textured surface and bevelled edges, and most visitors cannot tell it from timber without touching it.' },
      { q: 'Which is easier to fit yourself?', a: 'Both click systems are within reach of a careful beginner. Vinyl is easier to cut, as you can score it with a knife, while laminate needs a saw.' },
    ],
    citations: [cite('https://www.which.co.uk/reviews/flooring/article/flooring-types-explained', 'Which?: flooring types explained')],
    image: img('https://www.oakandloomflooring.example/images/samples.jpg', 'Sample planks of oak-effect laminate and vinyl laid side by side on a showroom floor'), author: null } as unknown as ArticleBody };

// 13 ── LEGAL / REGULATORY EXPLAINER
export const LEGAL: Art = { name: '13 legal explainer', site: 'https://www.keystonelettings.example',
  brief: { targetQuery: 'landlord electrical safety certificate', searchIntent: 'learn', relatedQuestions: ['Is an electrical safety certificate a legal requirement for landlords?', 'How often does a landlord need an EICR?', 'How much does a landlord electrical safety certificate cost?', 'What happens if a landlord does not have an EICR?'] },
  body: { title: 'The landlord electrical safety certificate: what the law requires', seoTitle: 'Landlord electrical safety certificate: the rules explained',
    metaDescription: 'Every rented home in England needs a landlord electrical safety certificate. Here is what the law requires, how often, what it costs and the fines.',
    excerpt: 'What the regulations ask of landlords, in plain terms.', slug: 'landlord-electrical-safety-certificate',
    intro: `If you let a home in England, you must have the wiring inspected by a qualified person at least every five years and give your tenants a copy of the report. Most people call that report a landlord electrical safety certificate. Its proper name is an Electrical Installation Condition Report, or EICR.\n\nThe rules have applied to every tenancy since April 2021. This guide explains what they require, what the report will say and what to do if yours comes back unsatisfactory.`,
    sections: sec([
      ['Is an electrical safety certificate a legal requirement for landlords?', `Yes. The Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020 apply to almost every private tenancy, including houses in multiple occupation. Regulation 3 says a landlord must "ensure that the electrical safety standards are met during any period when the residential premises are occupied under a specified tenancy".\n\nIn practice that means three duties. You must have the installation inspected and tested by a qualified person. You must get a written report with the date of the next inspection. And you must hand copies to the right people on time.\n\nA few lets are outside the rules, notably lodgers who share with their landlord and leases of seven years or more. Scotland has had its own, similar duty since 2015, and Wales brought one in under the Renting Homes Act in 2022.`],
      ['How often does a landlord need an EICR?', `At least every five years. The inspector can set a shorter interval if the installation is old or in poor condition, and the date on the report is the one that counts.\n\nYou do not need a new report each time the tenants change, as long as the current one is still in date. A new-build or a full rewire comes with an Electrical Installation Certificate, and that covers you for the first five years.`],
      ['Who gets a copy, and when', `The deadlines are short, and councils do check them.\n\n- Existing tenants: within 28 days of the inspection.\n- New tenants: before they move in.\n- Prospective tenants who ask: within 28 days of the request.\n- The local authority, if it asks: within 7 days.\n- The next inspector: a copy of the previous report.\n\nKeep proof that you sent it. An email with the report attached is enough.`],
      ['What the report says', `The inspector tests the fixed wiring: the consumer unit, cables, sockets, switches and light fittings. Appliances are not included. Each fault is given a code.\n\n- C1 means danger is present and somebody could be hurt now.\n- C2 means the fault is potentially dangerous.\n- FI means further investigation is needed without delay.\n- C3 means improvement is recommended, but it is not a failure.\n\nAny C1, C2 or FI makes the report "unsatisfactory". You then have 28 days, or less if the inspector says so, to have the work done. Afterwards you need written confirmation from the electrician, and you must send it to the tenant and the council within a further 28 days.`],
      ['How much does a landlord electrical safety certificate cost?', `It depends on the size of the property and the number of circuits. As a guide, a one-bedroom flat costs £120 to £180, a three-bedroom house £180 to £260 and a large house in multiple occupation £300 or more. Remedial work is extra. Replacing an old fuse board, which is the most common failure, costs £450 to £700.\n\nBe wary of very cheap offers. A proper inspection of a three-bedroom house takes three to four hours, and a report produced in forty minutes will not stand up if something goes wrong.`],
      ['What happens if a landlord does not have an EICR?', `The council can fine you up to £30,000 for each breach, and it can arrange the repairs itself and send you the bill. A missing report may also cause trouble with your insurer after a fire.\n\nThere is a practical cost as well. Mortgage lenders and letting agents now ask for the report as a matter of course, and we cannot advertise a property without one. Our [compliance checklist for landlords](https://www.keystonelettings.example/landlords/compliance-checklist) lists the other documents you need before a tenancy starts.`],
    ]),
    conclusion: `Put the renewal date in your diary six weeks ahead, use an electrician registered with NICEIC or NAPIT, and keep every report and receipt. The law is simple to comply with, and expensive to ignore.`,
    cta: { label: 'Talk to our lettings team', url: 'https://www.keystonelettings.example/contact' },
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is an EICR the same as a PAT test?', a: 'No. An EICR covers the fixed wiring. PAT testing covers portable appliances such as kettles, and it is good practice but not a legal requirement in England.' },
      { q: 'Does a C3 code mean I have failed?', a: 'No. A C3 is a recommendation. The report is still satisfactory, though it is sensible to plan the improvement.' },
      { q: 'What if the tenant will not let the electrician in?', a: 'Keep a record of every attempt to arrange access. A landlord who can show they took all reasonable steps is not in breach.' },
    ],
    citations: [cite('https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2020/312/contents/made', 'The Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020'), cite('https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/electrical-safety-standards-in-the-private-rented-sector-guidance-for-landlords-tenants-and-local-authorities', 'GOV.UK: guide for landlords on electrical safety standards')],
    image: img('https://www.keystonelettings.example/images/eicr.jpg', 'An electrician filling in a condition report next to an open consumer unit'), author: null } as unknown as ArticleBody };

// 14 ── SHORT NEWS UPDATE
export const NEWS: Art = { name: '14 short news update', site: 'https://www.pennineaccounts.example',
  brief: { targetQuery: 'Making Tax Digital for Income Tax', searchIntent: 'learn', relatedQuestions: ['Who has to use Making Tax Digital for Income Tax?', 'When does Making Tax Digital for Income Tax start?'] },
  body: { title: 'Making Tax Digital for Income Tax: who is next', seoTitle: 'Making Tax Digital for Income Tax: who joins in April 2027',
    metaDescription: 'Making Tax Digital for Income Tax reaches sole traders and landlords earning over £30,000 in April 2027. Here is what changes and what to do now.',
    excerpt: 'The second wave of sole traders and landlords joins in April 2027.', slug: 'making-tax-digital-for-income-tax-2027',
    intro: `The next stage of Making Tax Digital for Income Tax is five months away. From 6 April 2027, sole traders and landlords with income over £30,000 will have to keep digital records and send HMRC an update every quarter. The first group, those over £50,000, joined in April 2026.`,
    sections: sec([
      ['Who has to use Making Tax Digital for Income Tax?', `You are in the 2027 group if your combined turnover from self-employment and property was more than £30,000 in the 2025 to 2026 tax year. That is turnover before expenses, not profit. HMRC will write to you once your return for that year is filed, but do not wait for the letter to work out where you stand.\n\nThe threshold is due to drop again, to £20,000, in April 2028. Partnerships and limited companies are not affected yet.`],
      ['What changes', `The annual tax return goes. In its place you keep your records in approved software and send four short updates a year, due on 7 August, 7 November, 7 February and 7 May. A final declaration replaces the return and is due by 31 January, as now.\n\nNothing changes about when you pay. However, there are new penalty points for late updates, and four points bring a £200 fine.`],
      ['What to do now', `Check your turnover for last year, choose your software and start using it this winter, so the first real deadline is not your first attempt. If you would like us to send the quarterly updates for you, our [bookkeeping service](https://www.pennineaccounts.example/bookkeeping) includes them.`],
    ]),
    conclusion: `We are running free half-hour reviews for clients and non-clients until the end of March. Bring last year's figures and we will tell you which group you are in.`,
    cta: { label: 'Book a free review', url: 'https://www.pennineaccounts.example/mtd-review' },
    faqs: [
      { q: 'When does Making Tax Digital for Income Tax start?', a: 'It began on 6 April 2026 for income over £50,000. It extends to income over £30,000 on 6 April 2027 and over £20,000 on 6 April 2028.' },
      { q: 'Can I still use a spreadsheet?', a: 'Yes, if it is linked to HMRC by bridging software. Typing figures into a web form by hand is no longer allowed.' },
    ],
    citations: [cite('https://www.gov.uk/guidance/check-if-youre-eligible-for-making-tax-digital-for-income-tax', 'GOV.UK: check if you need to use Making Tax Digital for Income Tax')],
    image: img('https://www.pennineaccounts.example/images/laptop.jpg', 'A sole trader entering receipts into bookkeeping software on a laptop'), author: null } as unknown as ArticleBody };

// 16 ── SINGLE-WORD PHRASE (one main word: "what" and "is" carry no meaning)
export const ONEWORD: Art = { name: '16 explainer, one-word phrase', site: 'https://www.ashbylaw.example',
  brief: { targetQuery: 'what is conveyancing', searchIntent: 'learn', relatedQuestions: ['How long does conveyancing take?', 'How much does conveyancing cost?', 'Can I do my own conveyancing?'] },
  body: { title: 'What is conveyancing?', seoTitle: 'What is conveyancing? The legal side of moving, explained',
    metaDescription: 'What is conveyancing, and why does it take so long? A plain guide to the legal work behind buying or selling a home, stage by stage.',
    excerpt: 'The legal work that moves a home from one owner to the next.', slug: 'what-is-conveyancing',
    intro: `You have found a house, agreed a price and shaken hands. Then somebody says the word "conveyancing" and tells you to find a solicitor. So what is conveyancing? Put simply, it is the legal work that transfers ownership of a property from the seller to the buyer.\n\nIt starts when an offer is accepted and ends when the keys change hands and the new owner is registered. This guide explains what happens in between, how long it takes and what it costs.`,
    sections: sec([
      ['What a conveyancer actually does', `A conveyancer is a solicitor or licensed specialist who acts for one side of the sale. If you are buying, yours has three jobs: to check that the seller owns what they are selling, to uncover anything that might affect its value, and to make sure your money and your lender's money move safely.\n\nIf you are selling, the work is lighter. Your lawyer prepares the contract, sends the title documents, answers the buyer's questions and pays off your mortgage from the proceeds.\n\nMost of this happens out of sight, which is why the process can feel slow. A great deal of it is waiting for other people.`],
      ['The stages, in order', `Every sale follows roughly the same path.\n\n1. You instruct a conveyancer and prove your identity and the source of your funds.\n2. The seller's side sends the contract pack.\n3. Your conveyancer orders searches and raises enquiries.\n4. Your mortgage offer arrives and you sign the contract.\n5. Contracts are exchanged and the date is fixed.\n6. On completion day the money is sent and you collect the keys.\n7. The purchase is registered at HM Land Registry and any Stamp Duty is paid.\n\nThe third stage is usually the longest. Search results depend on the council, and replies to enquiries depend on the seller.`],
      ['How long does conveyancing take?', `Twelve to sixteen weeks is typical at the moment, from the offer being accepted to moving day. A purchase with no chain and no mortgage can be done in six. A leasehold flat in a long chain can take five months or more, because every link has to be ready on the same day.\n\nYou can speed your own part up. Return forms the day they arrive, send clear copies of your documents, and tell your lawyer at the start about anything unusual, such as a gifted deposit or building work without paperwork.`],
      ['How much does conveyancing cost?', `For a freehold house at the average price, expect legal fees of £1,000 to £1,800 plus VAT. Leasehold adds £300 to £600, because there is a lease to read and a managing agent to deal with. On top of the fee come disbursements, which are costs paid to others on your behalf. Searches are about £300, and Land Registry fees range from £20 to several hundred pounds.\n\nAsk for a fixed fee and a full list of extras before you instruct anyone. Our own [fee calculator](https://www.ashbylaw.example/fees) shows every item.`],
      ['Can I do my own conveyancing?', `Legally, yes, if you are a cash buyer. In practice very few people do. If you are taking out a mortgage, the lender will insist on a qualified conveyancer to protect its interest, so you would be paying for one anyway.\n\nThe work is also unforgiving. A missed restriction on the title or an unpaid service charge becomes your problem the day you complete, and there is no insurer standing behind you if you get it wrong.`],
    ]),
    conclusion: `In short, it is the careful, slow business of making sure that what you are paying for is really yours. Choose a firm that answers the phone, ask for a named contact and reply to their emails quickly. That does more to shorten the wait than anything else.`,
    cta: { label: 'Get a fixed-fee quote', url: 'https://www.ashbylaw.example/quote' },
    faqs: [
      { q: 'When should I instruct a conveyancer?', a: 'As soon as you put your home on the market or start viewing. Having one ready can save a fortnight once an offer is accepted.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between a solicitor and a licensed conveyancer?', a: 'A solicitor can practise in many areas of law. A licensed conveyancer specialises in property. Both are regulated and insured, and either can act for you.' },
      { q: 'Do I need a local firm?', a: 'No. Almost everything is done by email and post. What matters is that they are on your lender\'s panel and easy to reach.' },
    ],
    citations: [cite('https://www.gov.uk/buy-sell-your-home', 'GOV.UK: buying or selling your home')],
    image: img('https://www.ashbylaw.example/images/desk.jpg', 'A solicitor reviewing a title plan at a desk with a client'), author: null } as unknown as ArticleBody };

export const PILLAR: Art = { name: '15 long pillar', site: 'https://www.ridgelinelofts.example',
  brief: { targetQuery: 'loft conversion', searchIntent: 'learn', relatedQuestions: ['How much does a loft conversion cost?', 'Do you need planning permission for a loft conversion?', 'How long does a loft conversion take?', 'Is my loft suitable for conversion?', 'Does a loft conversion add value?'] },
  body: { title: 'Loft conversion: the complete guide for homeowners', seoTitle: 'Loft conversion guide: types, costs, rules and timescales',
    metaDescription: 'Thinking about a loft conversion? Our complete guide covers whether your loft is suitable, the four main types, costs, planning rules and how long it takes.',
    excerpt: 'Everything to know before you turn the roof space into a room.', slug: 'loft-conversion-guide',
    intro: `For most families who have outgrown their home, the cheapest extra bedroom is already there, sitting above the landing and full of Christmas decorations. A loft conversion turns that dead space into a proper room without giving up any of the garden, and usually without the upheaval of moving.\n\nWe have built more than four hundred of them across West Yorkshire since 2004. This guide sets out what we tell every homeowner at the first visit: whether the loft will work, what the options are, what it costs, which rules apply and how the build runs from week to week.`,
    sections: sec([
      ['Is my loft suitable for conversion?', `Most are, but three measurements decide it.\n\nThe first is head height. Stand under the ridge, which is the highest point of the roof, and measure down to the top of the ceiling joists. You want 2.2 metres or more. With less, the finished room will feel cramped once the floor is built up and the roof insulated, although there are ways round it.\n\nThe second is the pitch. A steeper roof gives more usable floor. Anything over 30 degrees is workable, and 40 is comfortable.\n\nThe third is the structure. Houses built before the mid-1960s generally have a traditional cut roof, with rafters and purlins and a clear space between them. These are the easiest to convert. Later houses often have trussed rafters, a web of thin timbers that fills the void. They can still be converted, but the trusses must be replaced with steel beams and new timbers, which adds cost.\n\nAlso look for obstacles: a water tank, a chimney breast or a boiler flue. All can be moved, and all should be priced at the start.`],
      ['The four main types', `Which one suits you depends on the shape of the roof, the budget and what the planners will accept.\n\n**Rooflight.** The roof stays as it is. Windows are fitted flush with the slope, the floor is strengthened and a staircase goes in. It is the cheapest and quickest option, and it works well where there is already generous head height.\n\n**Dormer.** A box-shaped structure is built out from the slope, usually at the back, with a flat roof and vertical walls. It adds a great deal of full-height floor area for the money, which is why it is the type we build most often.\n\n**Hip to gable.** On a semi-detached or end-terrace house with a sloping side roof, the slope is replaced with a vertical gable wall. It is often combined with a rear dormer to give a large bedroom and an en-suite.\n\n**Mansard.** The whole rear slope is rebuilt at a steep angle of about 72 degrees, with a flat roof on top. It gives the most space and suits terraced streets in towns, but it is the most expensive and almost always needs planning permission.`],
      ['How much does a loft conversion cost?', `Prices moved sharply between 2021 and 2024 and have been steadier since. For a typical three-bedroom semi in our area, these are realistic figures for a finished room, plastered and ready to decorate:\n\n- Rooflight: £28,000 to £40,000\n- Rear dormer: £45,000 to £65,000\n- Hip to gable with rear dormer: £58,000 to £80,000\n- Mansard: £70,000 to £95,000\n\nAdd £6,000 to £10,000 for an en-suite shower room. London and the South East are a quarter to a third higher.\n\nThe quote should include the structural engineer's calculations, building control fees, the staircase, electrics, heating, insulation, plastering and second-fix joinery. It usually leaves out decorating, flooring and fitted furniture. Ask what is excluded, and ask how much has been allowed for anything described as a "provisional sum".\n\nBe careful with a price that is far below the others. The saving nearly always comes out of the steelwork, the insulation or the fire protection, and those are the parts you cannot see once the plasterboard is on.`],
      ['Do you need planning permission for a loft conversion?', `Often not. Most houses have permitted development rights, which allow you to extend the roof without a planning application as long as you stay within limits. The main ones are:\n\n- no more than 40 cubic metres of added roof space on a terraced house, or 50 on a detached or semi-detached house\n- nothing higher than the existing ridge\n- no dormer on the slope that faces the road\n- materials that look similar to the existing house\n- side windows obscure-glazed and fixed shut below 1.7 metres\n- no balconies or raised platforms\n\nThose rights do not apply to flats or maisonettes, to listed buildings, or in conservation areas and national parks. Some newer estates had them removed when the houses were built, so check before you commit.\n\nEven where permission is not needed, we recommend applying for a lawful development certificate. It costs about £130, takes around eight weeks and gives you written proof for a future buyer's solicitor.`],
      ['Building regulations and the party wall', `Building regulations approval is always required, whatever the planners say. An inspector will visit at set stages to check the structure, the fire safety, the insulation, the stairs and the electrics.\n\nFire safety shapes the design more than people expect. Adding a storey to a two-storey house means the new room needs a protected route down to the front door. In practice that means fire doors on the rooms that open on to the stairs, mains-wired smoke alarms on every floor and, in some layouts, a sprinkler or mist system instead.\n\nIf you share a wall with a neighbour, the Party Wall Act applies as soon as steel beams bear on it. You must serve written notice at least two months before work begins. Most neighbours simply consent. If they do not, a surveyor draws up an award, at your expense, which is typically £900 to £1,500.`],
      ['The staircase', `Where the stairs go decides how good the new room feels, and how much you lose from the floor below. The best position is directly above the existing flight, so the new stairs rise from the landing and nothing is taken from a bedroom.\n\nThe regulations ask for 1.9 metres of headroom over the stairs, which can reduce to 1.8 at the side under a sloping ceiling. Where space is very tight, a narrower flight or alternating treads may be accepted for a single room. We would rather find a design that avoids both.`],
      ['How long does a loft conversion take?', `Allow three to four months from first visit to first night's sleep, and longer if planning permission is needed.\n\nDesign and paperwork come first. Measured drawings, structural calculations and a building regulations submission take four to six weeks. Party wall notices run alongside.\n\nThe build itself takes six to eight weeks for a rooflight scheme and eight to twelve for a dormer or a hip to gable. It runs in a fixed order. Scaffolding goes up and the roof is opened. Steel and new floor joists go in during the second week. Then the dormer is framed, covered and made watertight. Only then do we break through for the staircase, so for the first half of the job nobody needs to come through the house.\n\nFirst-fix electrics and plumbing follow, then insulation, plasterboard and plaster, and finally doors, skirting and the bathroom.`],
      ['Living through the work', `It is noisier than it is messy. Nearly everything arrives over the scaffold, and until the stairs go in the only sign indoors is the sound of feet overhead. Expect two or three dusty days when the opening is cut and the staircase is fitted.\n\nYou will not need to move out. The water or power may be off for a few hours on one or two days, and we always tell you the day before. Clear the loft completely before the start date, and warn the neighbours about the scaffold and the skip. A friendly word in advance saves a great deal of ill feeling later.`],
      ['Does a loft conversion add value?', `Usually, yes. Research by Nationwide found that an extra bedroom and bathroom in the roof can add around a fifth to the value of a three-bedroom house. The gain is greatest where the price gap between three and four bedrooms is wide, and smallest on streets with a firm price ceiling.\n\nValue is not the only test. Compare the cost with the cost of moving to a house with one more bedroom: stamp duty, agents' fees, legal fees and removals, all of which buy you nothing. For many of our customers that sum alone is more than the dormer.\n\nA conversion without paperwork can take value away. Buyers' solicitors ask for the building regulations completion certificate, and its absence delays or loses sales.`],
      ['Choosing a builder', `Look for a firm that does lofts week in, week out. The work is specialised, and a general builder who takes on one a year will be slower and will make more mistakes.\n\nAsk to visit a job in progress and one finished at least two years ago. Ask who draws the plans, who does the calculations and who deals with building control. Get the price, the payment stages and the start date in a written contract, and never pay more than a small deposit before work begins. You can see how we set ours out on the [how we work](https://www.ridgelinelofts.example/how-we-work) page.`],
    ]),
    conclusion: `A well-planned conversion is one of the few building projects that reliably gives a family more room for less than the cost of moving. Start with the head height, be realistic about the budget and get the paperwork right. The rest is carpentry.`,
    cta: { label: 'Book a free survey', url: 'https://www.ridgelinelofts.example/survey' },
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Can a loft with trussed rafters be converted?', a: 'Yes. The trusses are replaced with steel beams and new rafters, which adds roughly £4,000 to £7,000 to the cost and about a week to the programme.' },
      { q: 'How much head height do I need?', a: 'About 2.2 metres from the top of the joists to the underside of the ridge. Below that, a dormer or lowering the ceilings beneath may still make it work.' },
      { q: 'Will I need a new boiler?', a: 'Not usually. One extra radiator is well within the capacity of most boilers. An extra bathroom may mean a larger hot water cylinder or a more powerful combi.' },
      { q: 'Do I have to tell my insurer?', a: 'Yes, before the scaffold goes up. Most insurers continue cover during the work, but some add conditions and all of them need to know.' },
    ],
    citations: [cite('https://www.planningportal.co.uk/permission/common-projects/loft-conversion/planning-permission', 'Planning Portal: loft conversions and planning permission'), cite('https://www.nationwidehousepriceindex.co.uk/reports/home-improvements-that-add-value', 'Nationwide: home improvements that add value')],
    image: img('https://www.ridgelinelofts.example/images/dormer.jpg', 'A finished dormer bedroom under a sloping ceiling with two rooflights and oak flooring'), author: null } as unknown as ArticleBody };


